(`title`, `authors`, `abstract`, `email`, `year`, `volume`, `issue`, `pages`, `category`, `pdf_upload`)
VALUES
  ('Comments by the Editor-in-Chief', 'Prof. H.O. Otamere', '', 'otamereharrison@aauekpoma.edu.ng', 2023, 2, '1', '1', 'Editorial', 'uploads/AAUJMR_V2_I1/Paper 1. Comments by the Editor in cheif.pdf'),
  ('Effect of Ethanolic Seed Extract of Dacryodes Edulis on Kidney Function in Carbon Tetrachloride Induced Renal Toxicity in Rats', 'Eidangbe G.O., Obasi I.O., Eidangbe R.C.', 'This study determines serum creatinine, urea, electrolytes and kidney histology in carbon tetrachloride (CCl4) induced renal toxicity in rats. Dacryodes edulis (DE) seeds were collected and ethanolic extraction performed following standard laboratory procedures. Ninety a dult male rats (weighing 180 -200g) were distributed into 3 groups ; normal control, CCl 4 intoxication (intraperitoneal) without treatment (1ml/kg body of CCl 4; mixed in 1:1 olive oil every 72 hours for 3 doses) and the test group (CCl4 exposed treated with 1500 mg/kg DE extract). Six rats were sacrificed for blood collection by days 0, 7, 14, 21 and 28 post -treatment for estimation of serum urea, creatinine, sodium, potassium, chloride and bicarbonate ions . The kidney was harvested and fixed in 10% formalin by day 28 for histological processing. Data were analyzed using Statistical Package for Social Science software using one -way analysis of variance followed by Least Significant Difference test for comparison between groups at p < 0.05. CCl4 resulted in significant increase in serum urea and creatinine at baseline and DE treatment significant decrease in serum urea by day 7 and serum creatinine by 14 days post-treatment. Also, CCl 4 exposure caused a significant increase in serum Na+, K+ and Cl- and HCO3- at baseline. Treatment with DE did not significantly mitigate these electrolytes compared to control or untreated groups. The histological slides of group B and C showed vacuolations and defective glomeruli as compared to the normal glomeruli i n the control. These findings showed DE to have a weak action in mitigating CCl4 induced renal damage.', 'dreidangbegeorgeo@gmail.com', 2023, 2, '1', '2-8', '', 'uploads/AAUJMR_V2_I1/Paper 1. Eidangbe et al AAUJMR 2(1) 2-8.pdf'),
  ('Hepato-renal Function Assessment in Adult Male Wistar Rats Fed with White and Brown Species of Phaseolus Vulgaris L (Beans)', 'Otamere H.O., Ikhajiangbe H.I., Omodiagbe O., Shelu E.O., Ola-Ajayi T.O.', 'This study assessed the hepato-renal functions following consumption of white or brown species of Phaseolus vulgaris L (beans) in rats. Twenty-five adults male Wistar rats (body weight 100 ± 20g) were used for this study. The y were assigned to five groups as control fed normal rat chow (100g daily), groups 2 and 3 fed 25% and 50% white beans diet and groups 4 and 5 fed with 25% and 50% brown beans diet respectively for a 3 weeks duration. Blood samples were collected at day 22 and immediately centrifuged for estimation of serum Aspartate Aminotransf erase (AST) Alanine Aminotransferase (ALT), Alkaline Phosphatase (ALP), protein, Blood Urea Nitrogen (B UN) and creatinine, using commercial kits. The liver and kidneys were harvested for relative weight determination. The results showed that dietary consumption of white and brown beans modulates hepato-renal indices in a variety- and dose-dependent manner. While both bean varieties showed a general trend toward hepatoprotection, evidenced by reductions in AST, ALT, and ALP, higher levels of brown beans (50%) led to an increase in liver and kidney weights and a rise in ALT activity, suggesting potential hepatic stress. Renal function was largely preserved across all groups, with significant reductions in serum creatinine in rats fed white beans. In conclusion, this study suggests that moderate consumption of white beans may support hepato -renal health, while excessive intake of brown beans should be approached with caution.', 'otaharri2012@gmail.com', 2023, 2, '1', '9-14', '', 'uploads/AAUJMR_V2_I1/Paper 2. Otamere et al AAUJMR 2(1) 9-14.pdf'),
  ('Comprehensive Phytochemical Characterisation of Funtumia Elastica Root Ethanolic Extracts: Exploring Potentials for Male Fertility Enhancement', 'Eseine-Aloja C.E., Ugbeni O.C., Osubor C.C., Akpeh P.K.', 'Traditional medicinal plants, such as Funtumia elastica (F. elastica), are used ethnomedicinally to enhance male reproductive health, yet scientific evidence regarding their phytochemical composition remains limited. This study aimed to profile the phytochemical constituents of F. elastica root ethanolic extracts ( FEREE) and their potential implications for male fertility enhancement. Roots of F. elastica were collected, authenticated, and extracted using 40% ethanol. The percentage yield was determined, followed by qualitative and quantitative phytochemical screening. High -performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) was used to identify and quantify bioactive compounds. The percentage yield of FEREE was 5.38%. Qualitative analysis revealed the presence of coumarins, flavonoids, cardiac glycosides, steroids, and terpeno ids. Quantitative evaluation showed concentrations of coumarins (0.064 ± 0.005 mg/ml), flavonoids (0.303 ± 0.009 mg/ml), cardiac glycosides (0.554 ± 0.004 mg/ml), steroids (0.161 ± 0.009 mg/ml), and terpenoids (0.241 ± 0.007 mg/ml). HPLC analysis identifie d key bioactive compounds including quercetin, funtumine, kaempferol, hydrobenzoic acid, and roemerine. Many of these compounds possess antioxidant, anti -inflammatory, and endocrine -modulatory properties that may protect spermatogenic cells, improve sperm quality, and support hormonal balance. F. elastica roots are rich in bioactive phytochemicals with potential applications in male fertility enhancement.', 'cee-aloja@aauekpoma.edu.ng', 2023, 2, '1', '15-25', '', 'uploads/AAUJMR_V2_I1/paper 3. Eseine-Aloja AAUJMR 2(1) 16-25.pdf'),
  ('Laboratory-Processed Sorghum-Based Alcoholic Beverage does not Alter Liver Function in Adult Male Wistar Rats', 'Oyakhare M.O., Ikhajiangbe H.I., Adie P.U., Ogboh C.B.', 'This study investigates changes in selected liver function parameters following 14 days’ ingestion of laboratory processed sorghum based alcoholic beverage ( Burukutu) in adult male Wistar rats. Thirty (30) male Wistar rats weighing 80-100g were divided into 6 groups with group 1 as control, groups 2 and 3 received 5% of Burukutu every other day and on weekend respectively while groups 4, 5 and 6 received 1%, 5% and 25% of Burukutu daily. These treatments lasted for 14 days. At the end of treatment, blood sample was collected for assaying serum proteins and enzymes levels herein reported. The data was subjected to One Way Analysis of Varian (ANOVA) and p ≤0.05 was considered significant. The results showed that serum total protein, albumin, globulin, and liver enzymes; aspartate aminotransferase (AST), alanine aminotransferase (ALT) and alkaline phosphatase (ALP) activities in rats administered Burukutu were not signific antly different compared with the control. However, these liver proteins and enzymes increased in a dose dependent manner and are likely increased following every other day ingestion compared to weekend ingestions. The findings suggest that ingestion of la boratory processed sorghum based alcoholic beverage for 14 days may not affect the functionality of the liver in adult male Wistar rats. However, higher doses for a long duration is likely to result in a significant impact on the liver functionality and thus, the need for further studies.', 'oyakhireosenimusa@aauekpoma.edu.ng', 2023, 2, '1', '26-33', '', 'uploads/AAUJMR_V2_I1/paper 4. Oyakhare et al  AAUJMR 2(1) 26-33.pdf'),
  ('Evaluation of Digital Technology Applications in Anatomy Education and Impact on Academic Performance of Students', 'Vidona B.W., Oboh P., Ogbe O.C., Shelu O.E.', 'Anatomy education involves the study of the structure and function of the body, essential for health and medical professions. This study investigates the socio -demographic characteristics, digital technology usage, perceived effectiveness, and impact on academic performance among undergraduate students in Anatomy Education. A quantitative analysis was conducted with a sample predominantly aged 18 –22 years (65.9%) and a significant female representation (69.3%). Findings reveal that digital tools, particularly educational videos (48.9%) and 3D anatomy models (39.8%), are frequently utilized to enhance learning experien ces, with 28.4% of participants reporting frequent usage. A substantial proportion (45.3%) found digital tools helpful in understanding anatomical concepts, while nearly half (48.8%) reported significant improvements in academic performance attributed to t hese resources. The study highlights the transformative potential of digital technologies in anatomy education, emphasizing their role in fostering engagement, knowledge retention, and practical application of learning. Recommendations include integrating digital tools into curricula, enhancing faculty training, and promoting technological applications in health science education. Overall, the findings underscore the necessity for educational institutions to adapt to technological advancements to enrich stu dent learning and prepare them for future professional challenges.', 'ogbeonomeclementina@aauekpoma.edu.ng', 2023, 2, '1', '34-43', '', 'uploads/AAUJMR_V2_I1/paper 5.  Vidona et al AAUJMR 2(1) 34-43.pdf'),
  ('Anthropometric and Hematological Variables in Male Wistar Rats Fed with High Fructose Drink and Vitamin C', 'Omodiagbe O., Edwin E.E., Okonofua D.E., Imhantabhunu E.S., Omogbemhe S.A.', 'There is a global increase in fructose intake through sweetened beverages and h igh fructose consumption has been linked to metabolic disturbances. This study aimed to investigate the impact of vitamin C supplementation on anthropometric and hematological variables in rats fed a high - fructose drink. Twenty male Wistar rats were random ly divided into four groups ; g roup 1 (control), group two treated vitamin C (1g/100ml) , group three treated high-fructose drink (HFD) (15g/100ml), and group four treated high-fructose drink + vitamin C. At the end of the 28th day, anthropometric variables were measured, and blood samples were collected for analysis of hematological variables and indices. Statistical analysis was performed using one -way ANOVA with GraphPad at p ≤ 0.05. The results showed a significant increase in ant hropometric variables for the group fed HFD. This significant increase was observed in body weight ( 221.3 ± 4.06g vs 270.7 ± 12.17g), abdominal circumference (6.17 ± 0.33 vs 7.5 ± 0.29 cm ), and BMI ( 3.06 ± 0.06 vs 3.94 ± 0.24 g/cm2) compared to control. On the other hand, HFD non -significantly decreased red blood cell count, white blood cell count, platelet count, mean corpuscular hemoglobin and mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration while treatment with vitamin C improved these hematological parameters. This study therefore established that HFD may downregulate hematological parameters through increased body weight and abdominal circumference via lipogenesis and adiposity.', 'omoiyebagbe@aauekpoma.edu.ng', 2023, 2, '1', '44-49', '', 'uploads/AAUJMR_V2_I1/paper 6.  Omoiyebagbe et al AAUJMR 2(1) 44-49.pdf'),
  ('Liver Function and Oxidative Stress in Adult Male Wistar Rats Fed High Fat Diet', 'Osifo U.C., Ehinmen S.E., Amiendamhen I.F., Ekhosuehi O.', 'This study investigated the effects of high fat diet formulated from lard on some serum liver function parameters and oxidative stress in adult male Wistar rats. Twenty-four rats were divided into four groups; a control and three experimental groups fed diets containing 5%, 10%, or 25% lard -based diets for eight weeks. Blood sample was collected thereafter for the estimation of serum Aspartate Aminotransferase (AST), Alanine Aminotransferase (ALT), Alkaline Phosphatase (ALP) activitives and liver tissue lipid peroxidation (Malondialdehyde - MDA). The data were analsed for statistical significance at p <0.05 and presented in table. The results showed that fat diet caused a dose-dependent increase in the activities of serum liver enzymes and MDA level and were statistically elevated in the 10% and 25% fed groups compared with the control. These findings suggest that consumption of a high -fat diet s induced significant hepatocellular damage and oxidative stress, with the severity directly linked to the dietary fat contents.', 'osifocharity@aauekpoma.edu', 2023, 2, '1', '50-54', '', 'uploads/AAUJMR_V2_I1/Paper 7. Ehinmen et al AAUJMR 2(1) 50-54.pdf'),
  ('Assessment of Ethanolic Seed Extract of Garcinia Kola on the Brain of Lead Exposed Adult Male Wistar Rat', 'Omogbemhe S.A., Osifo U.C., Ighodaro C., Edwin E.E., Omodiagbe O., Imhantabhunu E.S.', 'Garcinia kola (G. kola) is a known potential source of antioxidant and anti-inflammatory agents. The present study investigated the effects of ethanolic extract of G. kola seed on lead -induced neurotoxicity in adult male Wistar rats. Sixty rats were randomly divided into 4 groups with group A as control. Groups B1, B2 and B3 were untreated lead-exposed (100 ppm), group C1, C2 and C3 were lead-exposed and treated with G. kola seed extract (150 mg/kg body weight) or while group D1, D2 and D3 were lead -exposed treated silymarin (150 mg/kg body weight) for 7, 14 and 21 days respectively. Data were analyzed using one-way ANOVA in GraphPad Prism version 10.4.0 at p value < 0.05. G. kola treatment significantly decreased brain tissue protein carbonyl by days 7 and 21, MDA by day 14 and GPx by day 21 while brain tissue superoxide dismutase and catalase activities were significantly increased by days 7 and 21 with G. kola treatment. This action of G. kola was similar with si lymarin. The findings indicate that G. kola contains essential bioactive compounds with antioxidant properties that help ameliorate the brain tissue Pb -induced oxidative stress.', 'omogbemhesunny@gmail.com', 2023, 2, '1', '55-61', '', 'uploads/AAUJMR_V2_I1/paper 8.  Omogbemhe et al AAUJMR 2(1) 55-61.pdf');
